// Signing configuration for Docky.
//
// These settings are deliberately absent from Docky.xcodeproj so that this
// file is the one place they are defined. Configure signing by editing
// Signing.local.xcconfig, never through Xcode's Signing & Capabilities tab —
// that editor writes literal values back into the project file and undoes
// this.
//
// The defaults below are ad-hoc, so a fresh clone builds with no Apple account
// and no membership in the upstream team. Apple recommends against ad-hoc for
// day-to-day work (TN3127), because an ad-hoc designated requirement is just a
// hash of the binary:
//
//   - Every rebuild that changes the binary reads as a new app to TCC, so
//     Docky's Screen Recording grant re-prompts and its Accessibility grant
//     silently stops working until it is toggled again in System Settings.
//   - Login-item registration (SMAppService) keys off the same unstable
//     identity, so launch-at-login can misbehave.
//   - Ad-hoc code under the hardened runtime normally fails library loading;
//     Docky gets away with it only because both targets already set
//     RUNTIME_EXCEPTION_DISABLE_LIBRARY_VALIDATION. Removing that would break
//     the ad-hoc default.
//
// So ad-hoc is the fallback that always builds, not the recommended setup. To
// sign with your own certificate, copy Signing.local.xcconfig.example to
// Signing.local.xcconfig and fill in your team. A free Apple ID is enough.
//
// The release script overrides all of this on the xcodebuild command line,
// which outranks every xcconfig.

CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = -
CODE_SIGN_STYLE = Manual
DEVELOPMENT_TEAM =

#include? "Signing.local.xcconfig"
